.TH FREEFALL 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
freefall \- park hard disk heads when a laptop accelerometer detects free fall
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B freefall
.RI [ device ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B freefall
is a daemon that protects a laptop hard disk from shock.
It waits for free-fall events from
.I /dev/freefall
and, on each event, parks the heads of the disk through the kernel's ATA
head-unload interface.
.PP
.I device
names the disk to protect and must have the form
.BI /dev/ name\fR,
where
.I name
is a whole disk listed in
.IR /sys/block .
The default is
.IR /dev/sda .
.B freefall
checks that
.I /sys/block/name/device/unload_heads
can be opened for reading; if it cannot, or if
.I device
does not begin with
.IR /dev/ ,
or if more than one argument is given, the program prints a usage
message and exits.
It then opens
.I /dev/freefall
and checks whether the LED file
.I /sys/class/leds/hp::hddprotect/brightness
exists; if it does not, the LED is not used.
.PP
The program then detaches from the terminal with
.BR daemon (3),
which changes the working directory to
.I /
and redirects standard input, standard output and standard error to
.IR /dev/null .
It opens the system log with the identifier
.BR "FREE FALL" ,
the facility
.B LOG_LOCAL1
and the process ID included in each message.
It requests the
.B SCHED_FIFO
scheduling policy at the highest priority and locks its memory with
.BR mlockall (2);
failures of these two requests are ignored.
.PP
The daemon then performs blocking one-byte reads from
.IR /dev/freefall .
Each read returns once one or more free-fall interrupts have occurred
since the previous read; the value read is not used.
For each read, the daemon:
.IP \(bu 3
writes 21000 to
.IR \%unload_heads ,
which parks the disk heads and blocks I/O to the disk for up to
21 seconds, and logs
.RB \(dq Parked
.IB device " disk head" \(dq
at priority
.BR LOG_INFO ;
.IP \(bu 3
writes 1 to the LED file, if it exists;
.IP \(bu 3
sets an alarm for two seconds later, replacing any pending alarm.
.PP
When the alarm expires, the daemon writes 0 to
.IR \%unload_heads ,
which unparks the heads and resumes normal operation, logs
.RB \(dq Unparked
.IB device " disk head" \(dq\fR,
and writes 0 to the LED file, if it exists.
Normal disk operation therefore resumes about two seconds after the most
recent free-fall event.
.PP
The daemon exits when a read from
.I /dev/freefall
fails, or when a write to
.I \%unload_heads
or to the LED file fails.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B freefall
exits with status 1 if the arguments are invalid, the disk's
.I \%unload_heads
file cannot be opened,
.I /dev/freefall
cannot be opened, or
.BR daemon (3)
fails.
Errors that occur after the program has detached are not reported to
the invoking process, and their messages go to
.IR /dev/null .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/freefall
Free-fall event device.
.TP
.I /sys/block/name/device/unload_heads
Head-unload control of the protected disk.
.TP
.I /sys/class/leds/hp::hddprotect/brightness
Disk protection LED, used if present.
.SH NOTES
.I /dev/freefall
is provided by the lis3lv02d accelerometer driver, which HP laptops use
through the hp_accel driver
.RB ( CONFIG_HP_ACCEL ),
and by the dell-smo8800 driver for Dell Latitude laptops
.RB ( CONFIG_DELL_SMO8800 ).
The lis3lv02d driver registers
.I /dev/freefall
only when the accelerometer has an interrupt line.
The hp_accel driver also provides the
.B \%hp::hddprotect
LED; see
.I \%Documentation/\:misc\-devices/\:lis3lv02d.rst
in the Linux source.
.PP
The kernel provides
.I \%unload_heads
for ATA disks; see
.I \%Documentation/\:admin\-guide/\:laptops/\:disk\-shock\-protection.rst
and
.I \%Documentation/\:ABI/\:testing/\:sysfs\-block\-device
in the Linux source.
At startup,
.B freefall
only checks that the file can be opened for reading.
If the disk does not support unloading its heads, the kernel rejects
the first park request with
.BR EOPNOTSUPP ,
and the daemon exits.
According to the kernel documentation, some disks support unloading but
the kernel does not enable it by default; it is enabled by writing \-1
to
.IR \%unload_heads ,
which
.B freefall
does not do.
.PP
Each park request carries a 21-second timeout.
According to the kernel documentation, the kernel resumes normal
operation of the disk when that timeout expires, even if no unpark
request arrives.
.PP
The kernel creates
.I \%unload_heads
and the LED
.I brightness
file with mode 0644, so
.B freefall
needs root privileges to park the heads and switch the LED.
.SH EXAMPLES
Protect
.IR /dev/sda :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# freefall /dev/sda
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mlockall (2),
.BR sched_setscheduler (2),
.BR daemon (3),
.BR syslog (3)
.PP
